  "summary_md": "Margin protection, rather than top-line expansion, is the prevailing theme across today’s batch of furnishings, fixtures, and auto-parts companies as soft housing demand and tariff uncertainty linger. **COOK** and **LCUT** both converted revenue softness into earnings beats through aggressive cost control, while **AMWD**, **HBB**, **HOFT**, and **PRTS** all ran self-help efficiency playbooks to stabilize margins despite fragile consumer spending. In automotive, aftermarket names **SMP** and **MPAA** are benefiting from steady replacement demand, while commercial-vehicle suppliers **SRI** and **CVGI** still wrestle with cyclical losses. The line between recovery and relapse remains thin: **FLXS** and **LOVE** are gaining share in a weak home-furnishings market, but weather and confidence wobbles introduced fresh uncertainty at **BSET** and **VIRC**.\n\nThe batch’s clearest outliers sit at the extremes. **SNBR** entered Chapter 11, rendering equity virtually worthless, while **ATER** is executing a full strategic exit and returning cash to shareholders through a novel contingent value right. A second cluster is making radical pivots out of legacy manufacturing into AI-adjacent or energy-driven narratives—**HYLN** abandoned hybrid trucks to become an AI-data-center power play, and **MWYN** and **XWIN** launched AI infrastructure ventures—bringing high execution risk alongside speculative enthusiasm. On the deal front, **THRM** announced a transformative merger targeting a doubling of EBITDA, and **MVST** faces a make-or-break factory ramp under a going-concern warning.",
  "summary_date": "2026-06-19"
